  "an3.60:2.4": "",
  "an3.60:2.5": "The Commentary divides this phrase into two: the “unexcelled” refers to nibbāna; the footing <i>(ogādha)</i> in the holy life refers to the path of arahantship. The analogy here is that of crossing a stream: as one approaches the far shore, one reaches a point where one can touch bottom and gain a footing; even though one is not yet on the shore, one is safe from being swept away. Throughout the Canon, however, the word <i>ogādha</i>—“gaining a footing,” “coming ashore”—is frequently used in connection with unbinding and the deathless. See in particular <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an7.15'>AN 7:15</a>.",
  "an3.60:2.6": "",
  "an3.60:2.7": "<i>Tathattā</i> literally means “thus-ness.” On the thus-ness of the Tathāgata, see <a href='https://suttacentral.net/iti112'>Iti 112</a>.",
